;0;;T;std.db.a sandbox nested in a transaction keeps the transaction's rollback;0;;T;std.db.is_retryable names the two the server is telling the program to try again;0;;T;std.db.create_schema renders one statement per table and one per sequence;0;;T;std.db.a where, an order by, a limit and an offset;0;;T;std.db.a comparison with null is unknown, and a where keeps only what is true;0;;T;std.db.nulls order last under asc and first under desc, and the words override it;0;;T;std.db.insert answers a count, and returning answers the rows;0;;T;std.db.a numeric column stores the column's scale and refuses what will not fit;0;;T;std.db.a constant that will not fit the column fails even when no row matches;0;;T;std.db.the four constraint classes carry the codes and the names postgres sends;0;;T;std.db.two nulls in a unique column do not collide;0;;T;std.db.a check passes on unknown and fails only on false;0;;T;std.db.a parent with a child cannot be deleted;0;;T;std.db.a parameter carrying SQL is a value and never syntax;0;;T;std.db.a committed scope is visible and an aborted one is not;0;;T;std.db.a statement that failed inside a scope makes every later one 25P02;0;;T;std.db.rolling back to a savepoint clears the failed state and the inner writes;0;;T;std.db.releasing a savepoint in an aborted transaction is 25P02 and commits nothing;0;;T;std.db.a sequence is not rolled back;0;;T;std.db.a write in a read-only scope is 25006 and a nested begin at another level is 25001;0;;T;std.db.the savepoint stack is bounded;0;;T;std.db.what the twin does not model, it refuses by name;0;;T;std.db.a comment and a result-column alias are ordinary SQL rather than a refusal;0;;T;std.db.an unknown relation and an unknown column are named;0;;T;std.db.a text parameter in an integer column is refused rather than coerced;0;;T;std.fs.a handled fs read is det, hermetic and answers the twin;0;;T;std.fs.a handled fs write is det and the next read sees it;0;;T;std.fs.the twin treats a path that leaves its root as naming nothing;0;;T;std.fs.one directory, never its subtree;0;;T;std.fs.a write stamps, a read does not, and a later write stamps later;0;;T;std.fs.a write needs its directory, and create_dir makes every missing one;0;;T;std.fs.remove takes one file or one empty directory and never a tree;0;;T;std.fs.rename is a move, and it refuses what it cannot place;0;;T;std.fs.a path spelled two ways is one file;0;;T;std.hash.the empty input;0;;T;std.hash.one byte;0;;T;std.hash.a hash is thirty-two bytes whatever the input;0;;T;std.hash.the block boundary is where a transcription goes wrong;0;;T;std.hash.a second chunk is a tree, and a tree is not a longer chunk;0;;T;std.http.a well-formed request line is split into its parts;0;;T;std.http.a token-valid method this server does not know reaches routing;0;;T;std.http.a request line that is not `METHOD SP TARGET SP VERSION` is refused;0;;T;std.http.an absolute-form target's authority is the request's, and Host stays visible;0;;T;std.http.a target that is not origin-form is refused rather than ending the run;0;;T;std.http.whitespace inside a request target is refused;0;;T;std.http.an absolute-form target carrying userinfo is refused;0;;T;std.http.a request target carrying a fragment is refused;0;;T;std.http.a request line over the bound is 414 and is not buffered past it;0;;T;std.http.a line ends with CRLF and with nothing else;0;;T;std.http.obsolete line folding and whitespace before a colon are refused;0;;T;std.http.a head that has not finished arriving is incomplete rather than refused;0;;T;std.http.an HTTP/1.1 request carries exactly one Host;0;;T;std.http.a duplicate Content-Length is refused even when the values agree;0;;T;std.http.a Content-Length is one or more ASCII digits and nothing else;0;;T;std.http.a Content-Length over the bound is 413 before a body byte is read;0;;T;std.http.Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding together is refused;0;;T;std.http.chunked must be the final transfer coding and may be applied once;0;;T;std.http.Content-Encoding is passed through untouched;0;;T;std.http.a length-framed body stops at its length and leaves the rest;0;;T;std.http.a body arriving in pieces decodes to the same bytes;0;;T;std.http.a chunked body decodes, and every malformed chunk is refused;0;;T;std.http.a chunk size that does not fit in an Int is refused rather than overflowing;0;;T;std.http.a chunk over the size bound and a body over the length bound are 413;0;;T;std.http.a chunk-size line over the bound is 400;0;;T;std.http.trailers are exposed separately and never merged into headers;0;;T;std.http.a body that stops mid-chunk is truncated rather than accepted short;0;;T;std.http.a header block over its bounds is 431;0;;T;std.http.Expect is 100-continue or 417;0;;T;std.http.keep-alive is the version's default unless Connection says otherwise;0;;T;std.http.a repeated field keeps every line in order and combines nothing;0;;T;std.http.a field value that is not text is refused rather than decoded lossily;0;;T;std.http.no accepted head admits two framings;0;;T;std.http.consumed is where the body begins, so a pipelined request is a slice;0;;T;std.http.a response carries exactly one framing field;0;;T;std.http.a 405 built here carries the Allow field it must;0;;T;std.http.1xx, 204 and 304 carry no body and no Content-Length;0;;T;std.http.a HEAD response carries Content-Length and no body;0;;T;std.http.a response's header order is a function of its value;0;;T;std.http.a chunked response is framed by Transfer-Encoding and nothing else;0;;T;std.http.a 1.1 connection is reused and its requests are answered in order;0;;T;std.http.Connection: close, HTTP/1.0 and the keep-alive bound each end the connection;0;;T;std.http.a body the handler ignores is drained before the next request is framed;0;;T;std.http.a chunked request is decoded and its trailers reach the handler separately;0;;T;std.http.a header timeout is 408, a body timeout is 408, and an idle timeout writes nothing;0;;T;std.http.the whole head is bounded by header_timeout_ms and not by each read;0;;T;std.http.a head arriving in pieces is read to its terminator;0;;T;std.http.a framing refusal is answered once and the connection is not reused;0;;T;std.http.a 100-continue is written before the body is read;0;;T;std.http.a streamed response is chunk-framed and ends with the terminating chunk;0;;T;std.http.a streamed response stops at a dead peer rather than writing into it;0;;T;std.http.a streamed response always ends with its terminating chunk;0;;T;std.http.a streamed response to an HTTP/1.0 peer is delimited by the close;0;;T;std.json.every kind of value parses;0;;T;std.json.whitespace is allowed everywhere between tokens and nowhere inside one;0;;T;std.json.a nested document parses to the value it describes;0;;T;std.json.an object is a map, so key order is the map's and not the document's;0;;T;std.json.a repeated key keeps the last value;0;;T;std.json.every escape form decodes;0;;T;std.json.a \u escape covers all four UTF-8 lengths;0;;T;std.json.a surrogate that is not part of a pair is refused;0;;T;std.json.a malformed escape names itself and its position;0;;T;std.json.an unescaped control character is refused and an escaped one is not;0;;T;std.json.a string that is never closed is refused rather than truncated;0;;T;std.json.a document that is not UTF-8 is refused whole;0;;T;std.json.escaping is the inverse of parsing, including the characters JSON does not require escaped;0;;T;std.json.runs and the escapes that end them survive the accumulator, byte for byte;0;;T;std.json.the number grammar is JSON's and not a decimal parser's;0;;T;std.json.a number that JSON does not admit is refused with a position;0;;T;std.json.a number outside Decimal's range is a decode error naming the byte offset;0;;T;std.json.a number keeps the scale it was written with;0;;T;std.json.trailing data after a complete value is refused;0;;T;std.json.a truncated document is a diagnostic and not a panic;0;;T;std.json.the shapes a lenient parser would accept are refused;0;;T;std.json.nesting is bounded, and the bound is a diagnostic rather than a stack overflow;0;;T;std.json.the leaf codecs round-trip;0;;T;std.json.a number with a fraction is not an Int;0;;T;std.json.a finite Float round-trips, and a non-finite one has no encoding to make;0;;T;std.json.a codec that is handed the wrong shape says which shape it wanted;0;;T;std.json.a failure inside a structure names its path;0;;T;std.json.a missing field and an optional one are different answers;0;;T;std.json.a list codec round-trips through the wire form;0;;T;std.json.a string-keyed map is an object and any other map is an array of pairs;0;;T;std.json.a variant with no fields is a string and one with fields is a tagged object;0;;T;std.json.a result codec round-trips both sides;0;;T;std.json.an option is null, and null is an option;0;;T;std.json.bytes are base64, padding included;0;;T;std.json.base64 that is not base64 is a decode error;0;;T;std.json.a realistic order payload parses, reads and re-renders;0;;T;std.json.a large array renders and parses back to itself;0;;T;std.json.rendering is canonical, so a rendered document parses to itself;0;;T;std.net.drain stops at end of stream rather than at the first short read;0;;T;std.net.a deadline is not an ending;0;;T;std.net.send_all loops over a partial write and stops at a dead peer;0;;T;std.router.the table this module tests with is unambiguous and well formed;0;;T;std.router.a literal path matches its route;0;;T;std.router.a parameter captures one segment and is typed by the pattern;0;;T;std.router.a parameter that does not parse is not a match;0;;T;std.router.a wildcard captures the rest, joined, and zero segments is enough;0;;T;std.router.a wildcard capture cannot say where its segments were;0;;T;std.router.literal beats typed beats parameter beats wildcard, left to right;0;;T;std.router.precedence is decided at the first position where two patterns differ;0;;T;std.router.a longer pat